      {
        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "Since there's no index file or module mapping in this repo, the \"agentic-3js-builder\" package import wouldn't resolve, so I should use relative imports instead, pointing to the actual builder, frame, math, path, paint, texture, and kit modules that exist in the repo structure.\n\nNow I'm designing a LoL-style treasure hoard with Hextech gold and blue/magenta glow accents, planning a root joint since the builder needs one even without articulated joints, and sketching rough dimensions for a chest (~0.9m wide) surrounded by coins, gems, goblets, and a crown, totaling roughly 2.2m by 1.3m footprint. I'm laying out the chest position and rotation in world space, thinking through how Euler rotations work for the parts, and planning to write a fairly substantial module before checking it with typecheck and a preview render to validate the result.\n\nI'm working out a transform helper that converts local positions and rotations into world space using a matrix stack, so the whole chest group can be yawed as a unit, and figuring out how scale, rotation, and position compose correctly in the builder's part function. I'm also planning the texture and color budget — keeping flat colors around 30, using painted gold for metal bands and coins, and starting to sketch SVG textures like a hextech rune emblem for coin faces.\n\nFor additional textures, I'm adding a Demacian-style crest for the chest's front plate, a sparkle card for gem facets, a rune band trim for the chest lid, and possibly a draped cloth with gold trim, plus a crystal glow gradient, while skipping extras like coin edge reeding or a scroll. For paint styles, I'm planning cel-shaded gold and wood-grain leather paints to match a painterly, hand-drawn League of Legends look with saturated colors and hue-shifted shadows.\n\nFor the gold paint itself, I'm defining it with a warm highlight tone and a cooler shadow tone, using mottling for texture variation, then thinking through part-count tradeoffs — since many small coins would eat into texture density, I'll keep background pile coins as flat-colored merged geometry while reserving textured treatment for hero coins. For the coin pile itself, I'm building a mound shape as a base, then scattering flattened cylinder \"coins\" across its surface with random spin and embedding, accepting the part-count increase that comes with it.\n\nRather than using individual stick calls per coin (which would blow past the part limit), I'm computing orientation matrices from each scatter hit's position and normal, then merging all transformed coin geometries by color group into a single part. Coins get random tilt off the surface normal, slight scale variation, and come in a handful of gold/silver tones to add visual variety to the pile.\n\nI'm also scaling up coin size for a chunkier stylized look and reducing cylinder segments to keep triangle counts manageable, plus adding a few neat stacks of coins near the chest and some spilling out toward the front.\n\nWith the lid hinged open wide, I'm piling up lumpy coin-mound shapes inside the chest that rise above the rim, then layering more ellipsoid lumps spilling over the front lip and spreading across the floor to the sides and back, roughly covering the area around the chest.\n\nWorking out the rotation math, I confirm θ should be about −1.8 radians (roughly 103°) so the front edge swings up and back correctly.\n\nNow I'm building transformation frames for the lid using matrix composition—translating to the hinge point and applying the rotation. I'm figuring out how to construct the lid's arch geometry, considering whether a cylinder segment rotated to align its axis with X, or a custom extruded half-disc shape, would best represent the closed arch form.\n\nI'm figuring out the lid's rotation angle so that when opened past vertical (around 103°), the underside's normal vector actually faces front-up, checking the trigonometry of the rotation about the X-axis to confirm the flat face orientation.\n\nWith that confirmed, I'm placing a thin crimson lining slab with gold studs just outside the arch's bottom plane, positioned slightly below the local origin so it reads as the inside lining visible when the lid swings open on its back-edge hinge.\n\nFor the chest body itself, I'm building wood-grain sides with gold corner posts, horizontal gold bands, and a front gold plate with lock and emblem detailing, plus rim trim—keeping the body open so the dark red lining sits just below the rim with the coin mound piled above it.\n\nI'm positioning the coin heap to overflow above the rim, resting the chest on four small gold feet at floor level with no plinth, and placing a tilted regal crown atop the left side of the coin pile.\n\nFor the crown itself I'm designing a \"Fallen King\" style piece: a gold band with alternating ruby and sapphire gems, eight spiked points tipped with pearls, arching torus bands meeting at a cross-topped orb, and a red velvet cap visible inside — scaled up stylistically to about 0.36 diameter and 0.3 height for visual impact.\n\nI'm placing it on the tallest heap, tilted about 15 degrees, and setting up a frame helper for positioning plus a lathe geometry helper to build the ring profile with flat faceting for that painterly, faceted LoL aesthetic. I'm weighing whether to lean on SDK extrude/lathe/sweep helpers versus raw geometry construction.\n\nDeciding to mix both: SDK extrude for crown spikes and emblem plates, SDK lathe for goblets using direct at/axis placement, and my own frame helper for tilted pieces like the crown and chest where I need computed directional vectors — sketching out a small Frame3 class to hold the transform matrix.\n\nNow I'm designing the goblets themselves: three or four variants (tall gold chalice with ruby gems, a jeweled silver one, a toppled one spilling coins, one stacked), working out the lathe profile with foot, stem, knop, flaring bowl and lip, plus a ring of small octahedra gems studded around the bowl band.\n\nFor the loose gems scattered around, I'm varying cut shapes—elongated octahedrons, icosahedrons, and a custom brilliant-cut lathe profile—across ruby, sapphire, emerald, amethyst, citrine, and hextech-cyan colors with glow materials, including a few larger hero gems and sparkle card accents above some of them.\n\nI'm also thinking of adding a hextech touch to tie into League identity—maybe a spiky cluster of cyan and magenta crystals glowing behind the chest.\n\nTo round out the hoard, I'm adding an embedded glowing gemstone on the chest's front plate and hexagon-patterned trim with gold wing motifs on the bands. I might also drape a pearl strand or add a small stack of gold bars for extra treasure variety, though these are optional flourishes beyond the core coins/gems/chest/crown/goblets.\n\nI'm also designing a crimson velvet cushion with tasseled corners to rest the crown on atop the heap, building it as a beveled extruded rounded-rectangle shape for that soft cushioned look.\n\nNow I'm planning out the overall scene layout, deciding where the chest and other elements sit across the world space.\n\nOpening the lid to roughly 100° puts its top edge near y=1.06, with the arched back bulging behind the chest and the flat front face showing the lining toward the viewer, giving a total chest height around 1.1m.\n\nNow I'm defining coin lumps spilling both inside the chest and onto the surface in front, setting up ellipsoid shapes with position and radii in world coordinates, applying the chest's yaw rotation so the interior lump aligns with the chest frame while the front spill pile sits larger and slightly skewed outside it.\n\nI'm placing additional heap lumps to the right (a crown cushion piling up), left, and two smaller ones tucked behind the chest for variety. Since ellipsoid centers sit at y=0 with half the shape underground, I'll need to filter scatter hits to avoid picking the underside—keeping only points with normal.y above about 0.15 and y above a small threshold so coins render only on visible upward-facing surfaces near the floor.\n\nNow I'm planning the mound base color as a flat dark old gold, then scattering coin positions across the lump surfaces with a minimum spacing, while being careful that coins placed inside the chest's interior lump end up hidden behind the box mesh—wasteful but acceptable since they won't be visible anyway.\n\nI'm working out a filter to exclude chest-interior hits using local-space coordinates, then setting coin orientation along the surface normal with some random tilt, slight offset to avoid z-fighting, random spin, and a scale range for variety.\n\nNow I'm picking a palette of gold-ish coin colors plus rare silver/pale variants, and checking whether I can merge geometries by color group using BufferGeometryUtils from three/addons to reduce draw calls.\n\n",
